<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (However, this raises a significant philosophical problem known as the "black box.", meaning ideas that are too complex or abstract for humans to understand fully, or with speed. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
486 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (Despite the philosophical discomfort of losing human-readable understanding, the overall impact of AI on mathematics is overwhelmingly positive; it forces a necessary trade-off that allows humanity to solve existential, hyper-dimensional problems that would otherwise remain forever out of reach.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
487 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (One such parallel from the past can be drawn to the historical controversy surrounding the Four Colour Theorem in 1976. When mathematicians Kenneth Appel and Wolfgang Haken set out to prove that any two-dimensional map could be coloured using only four colours without having adjacent regions share a shade, they ran into a problem of overwhelming scale. Rather than relying on a single elegant formula, they reduced the infinite possibilities down to 1482 distinct configurations. As manually checking thousands of complex map configurations exceeded human capabilities within a reasonable timeframe, Appel and Haken ran the exhaustive verification in a supercomputer.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
