617 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 371 0 R
/T (In earlier times, Central Asia was dry and arid. Because of this, rainfall was insufficient to support large-scale cultivation of water-intensive crops such as cotton. There were only two major rivers in the region, known as the Amu Darya and Syr Darya, which supplied the Aral Sea. Then, after the Soviet Union started to take control of Central Asia, the government started to see the region as an opportunity for agricultural development, especially cotton production. The government thought that cotton production was economically and industrially valuable, considering the suitable conditions in the region to grow it, except for the lack of rainfall. For this purpose, the Soviet Union came up with an idea of engineering the environment by bringing water to agricultural land.)
/E (In earlier times, Central Asia was dry and arid. Because of this, rainfall was insufficient to support large-scale cultivation of water-intensive crops such as cotton. There were only two major rivers in the region, known as the Amu Darya and Syr Darya, which supplied the Aral Sea. Then, after the Soviet Union started to take control of Central Asia, the government started to see the region as an opportunity for agricultural development, especially cotton production. The government thought that cotton production was economically and industrially valuable, considering the suitable conditions in the region to grow it, except for the lack of rainfall. For this purpose, the Soviet Union came up with an idea of engineering the environment by bringing water to agricultural land.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
618 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 371 0 R
/T <>
/E <>
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
619 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 371 0 R
/T (The 1960s was the main turning point for the project planned by the Soviets. It was an age of intensification of the irrigation project and cotton production. This converted vast areas of Central Asia into farmland. Studies made by the FAO also indicate that irrigated land increased from 4.5 million hectares to 7 million hectares between 1960 and 1980. At this point, the consequences became easily visible when the water volume in the Aral Sea started decreasing. Since the two main rivers of the region had their water redirected away from the sea, there was significantly less water flowing into the Aral Sea. With the contribution of the very dry climate in the region and the irrigation project, the water in the sea started to evaporate, causing it to become much smaller than before.)
/E (The 1960s was the main turning point for the project planned by the Soviets. It was an age of intensification of the irrigation project and cotton production. This converted vast areas of Central Asia into farmland. Studies made by the FAO also indicate that irrigated land increased from 4.5 million hectares to 7 million hectares between 1960 and 1980. At this point, the consequences became easily visible when the water volume in the Aral Sea started decreasing. Since the two main rivers of the region had their water redirected away from the sea, there was significantly less water flowing into the Aral Sea. With the contribution of the very dry climate in the region and the irrigation project, the water in the sea started to evaporate, causing it to become much smaller than before.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
