/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 380 0 R
/T ( In Nigeria, nearly half of communities in the Niger Delta receive fewer than two hours of electricity per day, despite being connected to the regional power grid \(Stakeholder Democracy Network, 2016\). Beyond the Delta, the problem is even more widespread: the Nigerian government estimates that more than 80 million people nationwide lack reliable access to electricity \(Stakeholder Democracy Network, 2016\). As a result, many households rely on kerosene lamps, diesel generators, and wood fires for light and energy, all of which release pollutants and increase the risk of respiratory illness. )
/E ( In Nigeria, nearly half of communities in the Niger Delta receive fewer than two hours of electricity per day, despite being connected to the regional power grid \(Stakeholder Democracy Network, 2016\). Beyond the Delta, the problem is even more widespread: the Nigerian government estimates that more than 80 million people nationwide lack reliable access to electricity \(Stakeholder Democracy Network, 2016\). As a result, many households rely on kerosene lamps, diesel generators, and wood fires for light and energy, all of which release pollutants and increase the risk of respiratory illness. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [3]
