701 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 382 0 R
/E ( Solar lanterns have a substantially smaller environmental footprint than kerosene lamps. Burning kerosene releases carbon monoxide, sulfur and nitrogen oxides, and black carbon \(Lam et al., 2012\). Black carbon is particularly damaging because it absorbs sunlight and rapidly warms the atmosphere. Research indicates that, although black carbon remains in the atmosphere for less time than carbon dioxide, it produces an especially intense warming effect \(Tesden, 2013, p. 3\). Because off-grid lighting remains common across South Asia and sub-Saharan Africa, replacing fuel-burning lamps with solar alternatives could help reduce the approximately 270,000 metric tons of black carbon produced by kerosene lighting worldwide each year \(Tesden, 2013, p. 3\).)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
