707 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 383 0 R
/E ( It is important to note that solar lanterns cannot fully compensate for unreliable electrical grids or provide enough energy to power entire homes or businesses. Still, their overall net impact is clearly positive. By combining silicon photovoltaic cells, rechargeable batteries, and energy-efficient LEDs in one portable device, engineers transformed several earlier scientific advances into a solution for inadequate access to electricity. In Nigeria, India, Cambodia, and Uganda, solar lanterns reduced exposure to hazardous particles, lowered carbon emissions, and created the infrastructure that people need to work safely and more productively. The success of solar lanterns in these countries demonstrates how a relatively small technology can have significant health, economic, and environmental benefits, especially in underserved regions. Studying the history and engineering behind solar lanterns suggests that some of the greatest scientific innovations stem from developing alternatives to previous models in the service of building a healthier, more sustainable future for more communities.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
