/P 385 0 R
/T (HOW THE BODY RECOGNIZES SWEETNESS)
/E (HOW THE BODY RECOGNIZES SWEETNESS)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
721 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 385 0 R
/E (To really understand how these sweeteners changed the way we eat, it helps to look at how our bodies process taste in the first place. When we eat something sweet, tiny receptors on our tongue act like lock-and-key mechanisms. Regular sugar fits into these receptors, sending a quick signal to our brain that says, "Hey, this tastes great, and it's full of energy!" Two specific proteins, called T1R2 and T1R3, act as our taste receptors for sweetness during this process, as indicated in Figure 1 below \(Fernstrom et al., 2012\).)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
