726 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 386 0 R
/T (Artificial sweeteners are engineered to fit into those exact same receptor locks, but with two clever adjustments. First, synthetic compounds bind to our taste receptors far more tightly than natural sugar does. Because of that extra strength, food companies only need to use tiny amounts, sometimes up to tens of thousands of times less than real sugar, to give us that same sweet kick \(National Cancer Institute, 2023\). Second, compounds like saccharin are incredibly stable. Our digestive systems simply can't break down their unique chemical bonds \(del Pozo et al., 2022, p. 12\). Since our bodies can't digest them, they pass right through us unchanged. On the one hand, this means that the body cannot break these compounds down and use them for energy. On the other hand, these chemical properties meant that people living with diabetes or those trying to manage their sugar intake could enjoy sweet foods without worrying about dangerous spikes in blood sugar. )
/E (Artificial sweeteners are engineered to fit into those exact same receptor locks, but with two clever adjustments. First, synthetic compounds bind to our taste receptors far more tightly than natural sugar does. Because of that extra strength, food companies only need to use tiny amounts, sometimes up to tens of thousands of times less than real sugar, to give us that same sweet kick \(National Cancer Institute, 2023\). Second, compounds like saccharin are incredibly stable. Our digestive systems simply can't break down their unique chemical bonds \(del Pozo et al., 2022, p. 12\). Since our bodies can't digest them, they pass right through us unchanged. On the one hand, this means that the body cannot break these compounds down and use them for energy. On the other hand, these chemical properties meant that people living with diabetes or those trying to manage their sugar intake could enjoy sweet foods without worrying about dangerous spikes in blood sugar. )
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
727 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 386 0 R
/T (If artificial sweeteners were such a great solution for reducing sugar, where did things go wrong? The problem isn't necessarily the sweetener itself, but how the food industry began using it.)
/E (If artificial sweeteners were such a great solution for reducing sugar, where did things go wrong? The problem isn't necessarily the sweetener itself, but how the food industry began using it.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
728 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 386 0 R
/T (In traditional baking and cooking, real sugar does a lot more than just make items taste good. It adds bulk, texture, moisture, and structure to food. When you take sugar out, the food looks and tastes pretty unappealing. High-intensity sweeteners offered a sneaky workaround: manufacturers could use cheap chemical fillers, water, and industrial emulsifiers to rebuild the food's structure, and then add a tiny pinch of artificial sweetener to fix the flavor \(Seto et al., 2025, p. 2\).)
/E (In traditional baking and cooking, real sugar does a lot more than just make items taste good. It adds bulk, texture, moisture, and structure to food. When you take sugar out, the food looks and tastes pretty unappealing. High-intensity sweeteners offered a sneaky workaround: manufacturers could use cheap chemical fillers, water, and industrial emulsifiers to rebuild the food's structure, and then add a tiny pinch of artificial sweetener to fix the flavor \(Seto et al., 2025, p. 2\).)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
