/Title (This tension is clearest in recent AI breakthroughs involving the Navier-Stokes equations, one of the seven famous Millennium Prize Problems. These equations describe how fluids like air and water move, governing everything from climate patterns to turbulence around aeroplane wings. Because fluid motion is so chaotic, mathematicians have struggled to solve them for over two hundred years. Recently, swarms of AI agents operating autonomously have begun finding new patterns and potential solutions that humans missed. Unlike the 1976 supercomputer, which simply checked a list of maps built by humans, modern AI creates its own logical paths across high-dimensional spaces that human brains may struggle to visualise.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3129 0 R
/Next 3134 0 R
>>
endobj
3133 0 obj
<<
/Title (Artificial sweeteners are engineered to fit into those exact same receptor locks, but with two clever adjustments. First, synthetic compounds bind to our taste receptors far more tightly than natural sugar does. Because of that extra strength, food companies only need to use tiny amounts, sometimes up to tens of thousands of times less than real sugar, to give us that same sweet kick \(National Cancer Institute, 2023\). Second, compounds like saccharin are incredibly stable. Our digestive systems simply can't break down their unique chemical bonds \(del Pozo et al., 2022, p. 12\). Since our bodies can't digest them, they pass right through us unchanged. On the one hand, this means that the body cannot break these compounds down and use them for energy. On the other hand, these chemical properties meant that people living with diabetes or those trying to manage their sugar intake could enjoy sweet foods without worrying about dangerous spikes in blood sugar.)
/Dest [46 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3135 0 R
/Next 3130 0 R
>>
endobj
