3179 0 obj
<<
/Title (In earlier times, Central Asia was dry and arid. Because of this, rainfall was insufficient to support large-scale cultivation of water-intensive crops such as cotton. There were only two major rivers in the region, known as the Amu Darya and Syr Darya, which supplied the Aral Sea. Then, after the Soviet Union started to take control of Central Asia, the government started to see the region as an opportunity for agricultural development, especially cotton production. The government thought that cotton production was economically and industrially valuable, considering the suitable conditions in the region to grow it, except for the lack of rainfall. For this purpose, the Soviet Union came up with an idea of engineering the environment by bringing water to agricultural land.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3181 0 R
/Next 3177 0 R
>>
endobj
